-- S21 -- «B22»: THE TEXT OF AN ASSUMPTION IS ONE LINE LONG.
--
-- `FRAGMENTE.md` «B22»: *"Strings are single-line (`char` excludes `newline`).
-- All three `claim` texts and two `assume` texts are multi-line."* Cited at F2
-- and F6.
--
-- STILL OPEN, measured:
--
--   assume a "erste Zeile
--   zweite Zeile" falsifier p;
--     [L001] string literal with no closing quote
--
-- and there is no concatenation and no escape for a newline in the grammar
-- (`string`/`char`, `SYNTAX.md`:95). What a writer does instead is what this
-- file does and what `S04` did: put the whole sentence on one very long line.
-- The 60-odd assumption texts in the tree are all one line, some of them over
-- 200 characters, and `pruefe-englisch.py`/`-zeilen` read them as one.
--
-- WHY IT IS NOT COSMETIC. An assumption's text is the ONE place a reader
-- learns what is being trusted; `UNFALSIFIZIERBAR.md` is a whole document
-- about those sentences. A carrier that forces them onto one line makes the
-- long ones unreadable in a diff and invites the short ones.
--
-- The file below is accepted -- the refused shape is in the comment above,
-- because a file that does not lex says nothing else either.

module schreibprobe::zusicherungstext {

extern fn sonde_dma_kohaerent() -> bool effects { pure } costs <= 1 ops;

assume dma_ist_kohaerent
    "The device's DMA writes are coherent with the CPU's caches on this platform, so a descriptor the device wrote is visible to a plain load after the completion interrupt; where it is not, an explicit invalidate stands between them, and this program does not contain one -- that is the assumption, and the probe beside it reads the descriptor twice and compares."
    falsifier sonde_dma_kohaerent;

}
